Z||000036||maryjane||12-11-2001||11:12 PM||||Ray, I might be inclined to believe that, if it happened that way. Problem is, Enron dealt almost exclusively in NATURAL GAS and electrical power, with side ventures in steel and broadband. The largest producer of gasoline in the world is Exxon, with the largest proven crude oil reserves in the world. They own:<BR>1.Their own oil reserves.<BR>2.Their own ocean going tankers.<BR>2.Their own refineries.<BR>4.Their own pipelines and distribution   <BR>  centers.<BR>5 Their own truck tankers.<BR>6.Their own service stations.  <BR>Add to that, Year 2000 total income was 232 billion dollars, net-17.1 billion dollars-cash reserves-$15 million-total assests pushing  130 billion  dollars, they could at any time in the last 7 years, buy Enron 10 times over, and your explanation isn't plausible. Perhaps in natural gas or electrical power, but not in gasoline. BP Ammoco, Chevron/Texaco (2nd & 3rd in world)are also far too large to be influenced by a commodity trader. These three producers provide most of the gasoline in the U.S.. Even to your neighborhood convenience stores. You don't think 7-11 has their own refineries do you? Supply & demand set the price of gasoline, Ray. My family & I have been involved with Exxon since before it was Exxon (Humble Oil & Refining Co), I know what I'm talking about in this matter. Like I say, you may be right if you are referring to Nat Gas or electricty, even home heating oil, since they are much more volitile in price fluctuation and supply. But not gasoline. ||||reg||1
